    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">twenty</span>-<span class=\"vp-verb\">one</span> (21) · <span class=\"vp-prep\">thirty</span>-<span class=\"vp-verb\">four</span> (34) · two hundred <span class=\"vp-prep\">and</span> fifty (250)",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>0 to 12</strong> are single words, learn them one by one: <em>zero, one, two … twelve</em>. From 13 to 19 add <strong>-teen</strong>: <em>thir<strong>teen</strong>, four<strong>teen</strong></em>. Careful with <em>fifteen</em> and <em>eighteen</em>.",
        "tag": "0 to 19"
      },
      {
        "html": "The tens end in <strong>-ty</strong>: <em>twenty, forty, fifty</em>. Not: <em>fourty</em>. <strong>-teen</strong> and <strong>-ty</strong> sound alike, but the stress is different: <em>thirTEEN</em> (13) and <em>THIRty</em> (30).",
        "tag": "-teen or -ty"
      },
      {
        "html": "From 21 to 99 say the tens first, then the ones, and write a <strong>hyphen</strong>: <em>twenty-one, thirty-four, ninety-nine</em>.",
        "tag": "tens first"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Hundred</strong> and <strong>thousand</strong> never take an <em>-s</em> after a number: <em>two hundred people</em>, not <em>two hundreds people</em>. In British English we say <strong>and</strong> before the last part: <em>three hundred and fifty, nine hundred and ninety-nine</em>. One hundred is also <em>a hundred</em>.",
        "tag": "hundred, thousand"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "<strong>Prices</strong>: <em>£3.50</em> is <em>three pounds fifty</em>. <strong>Phone numbers</strong> are read digit by digit: 0 is often <em>oh</em>, and 33 is <em>double three</em>. <strong>Time</strong>: <em>7.30</em> is <em>half past seven</em>, <em>8.45</em> is <em>quarter to nine</em>. <strong>Years</strong>: <em>1990</em> is <em>nineteen ninety</em>, <em>2025</em> is <em>twenty twenty-five</em>. Ordinal numbers (<em>first, second</em>) are a separate topic.",
